Install AI SKILLUse MCP <p>Install the AI SKILL (for Copilot, Claude, Cursor and others) using skills.sh:</p> <pre><code>npx skills add CosminMihuMDC/KtorMonitor --skill ktormonitor
</code></pre> <p>Let AI agents (Copilot, Claude, Cursor, ...) query the calls captured by KtorMonitor at runtime, on any platform, independent of the HTTP client used.</p> <pre><code>dependencies {
    debugImplementation("ro.cosminmihu.ktor:ktor-monitor-mcp:1.18.1")
    releaseImplementation("ro.cosminmihu.ktor:ktor-monitor-mcp-no-op:1.18.1")
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>KtorMonitor.MCP.start {
    appName = "MyApp" // recommended: shows which app is which in list_apps
    // host = "127.0.0.1"
    // port = 2991
    // token = null
}
</code></pre> <p>See MCP for the companion JAR setup and connectivity per platform.</p>

Ktor - Kotlin MultiplatformKtor - AndroidOkHttp - Android &amp; JVMhttp4k - Android &amp; JVMMCP <pre><code>kotlin {
    sourceSets {
        commonMain.dependencies {
            implementation("ro.cosminmihu.ktor:ktor-monitor-logging:1.18.1")
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>For Release Builds (No-Op)</p> <p>To isolate KtorMonitor from release builds, use the <code>ktor-monitor-logging-no-op</code> variant. This ensures the monitor code is not included in production artifact.</p> <pre><code>kotlin {
    sourceSets {
        commonMain.dependencies {
            implementation("ro.cosminmihu.ktor:ktor-monitor-logging-no-op:1.18.1")
        }
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>dependencies {
    debugImplementation("ro.cosminmihu.ktor:ktor-monitor-logging:1.18.1")
    releaseImplementation("ro.cosminmihu.ktor:ktor-monitor-logging-no-op:1.18.1")
}
</code></pre> <p>For Android minSdk &lt; 26, Core Library Desugaring is required.</p> <pre><code>dependencies {
    debugImplementation("ro.cosminmihu.ktor:ktor-monitor-okhttp-interceptor:1.18.1")
    releaseImplementation("ro.cosminmihu.ktor:ktor-monitor-okhttp-interceptor-no-op:1.18.1")
}
</code></pre> <p>For Android minSdk &lt; 26, Core Library Desugaring is required.</p> <pre><code>dependencies {
    debugImplementation("ro.cosminmihu.ktor:ktor-monitor-http4k-filter:1.18.1")
    releaseImplementation("ro.cosminmihu.ktor:ktor-monitor-http4k-filter-no-op:1.18.1")
}
</code></pre> <p>For Android minSdk &lt; 26, Core Library Desugaring is required.</p> <p>Works alongside any of the HTTP clients above, on Android, iOS, Desktop and Web. Skip this unless you want an AI agent (Copilot, Claude, Cursor, ...) to query the captured calls. See MCP for the full setup (companion JAR, connectivity per platform, exposed tools).</p> <pre><code>dependencies {
    debugImplementation("ro.cosminmihu.ktor:ktor-monitor-mcp:1.18.1")
    releaseImplementation("ro.cosminmihu.ktor:ktor-monitor-mcp-no-op:1.18.1")
}
</code></pre>

Ktor Client PluginOkHttp Interceptorhttp4k FilterMCP <pre><code>HttpClient {

    install(KtorMonitorLogging) {  
        sanitizeHeader { header -&gt; header == "Authorization" }  
        filter { request -&gt; !request.url.host.contains("cosminmihu.ro") }  
        showNotification = true  
        retentionPeriod = RetentionPeriod.OneHour
        maxContentLength = ContentLength.Default
    }
}
</code></pre> <ul> <li><code>sanitizeHeader</code> - sanitize sensitive headers to avoid their values appearing in the logs</li> <li><code>filter</code> - filter logs for calls matching a predicate.</li> <li><code>showNotification</code> - Keep track of latest requests and responses into notification. Default is true. Android and iOS only. Notifications permission needs to be granted.</li> <li><code>retentionPeriod</code> - The retention period for the logs. Default is 1h.</li> <li><code>maxContentLength</code> - The maximum length of the content that will be logged. After this, body will be truncated. Default is 250_000. To log the entire body use <code>ContentLength.Full</code>.</li> </ul> <pre><code>OkHttpClient.Builder()
    .addInterceptor(
        KtorMonitorInterceptor {
            sanitizeHeader { header -&gt; header == "Authorization" }
            filter { request -&gt; !request.url.host.contains("cosminmihu.ro") }
            showNotification = true
            retentionPeriod = RetentionPeriod.OneHour
            maxContentLength = ContentLength.Default
        }
    )
    .build()
</code></pre> <ul> <li><code>sanitizeHeader</code> - sanitize sensitive headers to avoid their values appearing in the logs</li> <li><code>filter</code> - filter logs for calls matching a predicate.</li> <li><code>showNotification</code> - Keep track of latest requests and responses into notification. Default is true. Android and iOS only. Notifications permission needs to be granted.</li> <li><code>retentionPeriod</code> - The retention period for the logs. Default is 1h.</li> <li><code>maxContentLength</code> - The maximum length of the content that will be logged. After this, body will be truncated. Default is 250_000. To log the entire body use <code>ContentLength.Full</code>.</li> </ul> <p>Note</p> <p>Install it with <code>addInterceptor</code>, not <code>addNetworkInterceptor</code>. OkHttp does not run network interceptors for WebSocket calls, so a WebSocket handshake would not be logged. To also log WebSocket frames, open the socket with <code>client.newMonitoredWebSocket(request, listener)</code> instead of <code>client.newWebSocket(...)</code>. With <code>addInterceptor</code>, redirects are logged as a single call.</p> <p>Why <code>newMonitoredWebSocket</code>?</p> <p>An OkHttp interceptor only sees the WebSocket handshake (the <code>101</code> response). After the upgrade, frames no longer pass through <code>Interceptor.intercept</code>; OkHttp delivers received frames through <code>WebSocketListener</code> and sent frames through <code>WebSocket.send/close</code>. <code>newMonitoredWebSocket(request, listener)</code> wraps both the listener and the returned <code>WebSocket</code>, so it can:</p> <ul> <li>record sent and received messages and the close/failure events;</li> <li>link them to the <code>101</code> call already logged by the interceptor;</li> <li>set the connection status (open, closed, failed) and the close code.</li> </ul> <p>Without it, only the <code>101</code> call is shown, with no messages. Ktor does not need this, because the plugin hooks directly into the WebSocket session.</p> <pre><code>val webSocket = client.newMonitoredWebSocket(request, listener)
</code></pre> <pre><code>KtorMonitorFilter {
    sanitizeHeader { header -&gt; header == "Authorization" }
    filter { request -&gt; !request.uri.host.contains("cosminmihu.ro") }
    showNotification = true
    retentionPeriod = RetentionPeriod.OneHour
    maxContentLength = ContentLength.Default
}.then(JavaHttpClient())
</code></pre> <ul> <li><code>sanitizeHeader</code> - sanitize sensitive headers to avoid their values appearing in the logs</li> <li><code>filter</code> - filter logs for calls matching a predicate.</li> <li><code>showNotification</code> - Keep track of latest requests and responses into notification. Default is true. Android only. Notifications permission needs to be granted.</li> <li><code>retentionPeriod</code> - The retention period for the logs. Default is 1h.</li> <li><code>maxContentLength</code> - The maximum length of the content that will be logged. After this, body will be truncated. Default is 250_000. To log the entire body use <code>ContentLength.Full</code>.</li> </ul> <pre><code>KtorMonitor.MCP.start {
    appName = "MyApp" // recommended: shows which app is which in list_apps
    // host = "127.0.0.1"
    // port = 2991
    // token = null
}
</code></pre> <p>Call once (e.g. in debug builds only). Does nothing unless called. See MCP for the companion JAR setup and connectivity per platform.</p>
